{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-4555" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For $x>2$, find ${f}^{′}(x)$ if $f(x)={\\int }_{2}^{\\sqrt{x}}\\frac{1}{2-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057235/state.json" "file_sha256" "459b01f2922a2facdd7474498337b79a553fa0414a822134219f87714fad8561"} {"question" "q-4555" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute ${f}^{′}(x),$ we first make the substitution $u=\\sqrt{x}$ and then we apply the chain rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{2}^{\\sqrt{x}}\\frac{1}{2-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{2}^{u}\\frac{1}{2-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe integrand is continuous on $[2,u],$ and so by FTC II we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2-{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2-{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2-(\\sqrt{x})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}(2-x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057235/state.json" "file_sha256" "459b01f2922a2facdd7474498337b79a553fa0414a822134219f87714fad8561"} {"question" "q-4555" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057235/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "207539b50a0adc6d7c33b13e37a6eef48dd2e93a17a7afb2a2c5f7572dce0839"} {"question" "q-4555" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057235/state.json" "file_sha256" "459b01f2922a2facdd7474498337b79a553fa0414a822134219f87714fad8561"} {"question" "q-4555" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{2-\\sqrt{x}}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2-x}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}(2-\\sqrt{x})}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}(2-x)}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{2-\\sqrt{x}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057235/state.json" "file_sha256" "459b01f2922a2facdd7474498337b79a553fa0414a822134219f87714fad8561"} {"question" "q-4555" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}(2-x)}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057235/state.json" "file_sha256" "459b01f2922a2facdd7474498337b79a553fa0414a822134219f87714fad8561"} {"question" "q-75220" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(x)={x}^{2}+5x+1.$ Find ${g}^{′}(5)$ given the function is defined by $g(x)={\\int }_{x}^{7}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057235/state.json" "file_sha256" "459b01f2922a2facdd7474498337b79a553fa0414a822134219f87714fad8561"} {"question" "q-75220" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "In this example, the variable limit $x$ is located on the lower limit. To apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit. Using the properties of definite integrals, we can write\n\n$$\ng(x)={\\int }_{x}^{7}({t}^{2}+5t+1)\\,\\text{d}t=-{\\int }_{7}^{x}({t}^{2}+5t+1)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by FTC II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{x}^{7}({t}^{2}+5t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{\\int }_{7}^{x}({t}^{2}+5t+1)\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{7}^{x}({t}^{2}+5t+1)\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+5x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of ${g}^{′}(5),$ we substitute $x=5$ in the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(5) & =-({5}^{2}+5\\cdot 5+1) \\\\  & =-(25+25+1) \\\\  & =-51\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057235/state.json" "file_sha256" "459b01f2922a2facdd7474498337b79a553fa0414a822134219f87714fad8561"} {"question" "q-75220" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057235/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "207539b50a0adc6d7c33b13e37a6eef48dd2e93a17a7afb2a2c5f7572dce0839"} {"question" "q-75220" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057235/state.json" "file_sha256" "459b01f2922a2facdd7474498337b79a553fa0414a822134219f87714fad8561"} {"question" "q-75220" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{g}^{′}(5)=-49"] ["math" "{g}^{′}(5)=-51"] ["math" "{g}^{′}(5)=39"] ["math" "{g}^{′}(5)=43"] ["math" "{g}^{′}(5)=50"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057235/state.json" "file_sha256" "459b01f2922a2facdd7474498337b79a553fa0414a822134219f87714fad8561"} {"question" "q-75220" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{g}^{′}(5)=-51"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057235/state.json" "file_sha256" "459b01f2922a2facdd7474498337b79a553fa0414a822134219f87714fad8561"} {"question" "q-117291" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find ${f}^{′}(0)$ if $f(x)={\\int }_{x}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{e}^{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057235/state.json" "file_sha256" "459b01f2922a2facdd7474498337b79a553fa0414a822134219f87714fad8561"} {"question" "q-117291" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Here, both the lower and upper limits contain a variable. However, to apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit only. So, we can split the integral into two integrals, and rewrite them so that in both cases the variable is on the upper limit. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{x}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{e}^{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{x}^{a}{e}^{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{e}^{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{a}^{x}{e}^{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{e}^{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is any constant number between $x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\nThe integrand ${e}^{1-{t}^{2}}$ is continuous for all $t,$ so applying the FTC II gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{\\int }_{a}^{x}{e}^{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{e}^{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{x}{e}^{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{e}^{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-{e}^{1-{x}^{2}}+\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}({\\int }_{a}^{u}{e}^{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-{e}^{1-{x}^{2}}+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x){e}^{1-{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =-{e}^{1-{x}^{2}}-{e}^{1-{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =-{e}^{1-{x}^{2}}-{e}^{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\nFinally, we can compute ${f}^{′}(0).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & =-{e}^{1-{0}^{2}}-{e}^{{sin}^{2}⁡0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =-{e}^{1-0}-0 \\\\  & =-e.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057235/state.json" "file_sha256" "459b01f2922a2facdd7474498337b79a553fa0414a822134219f87714fad8561"} {"question" "q-117291" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057235/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "207539b50a0adc6d7c33b13e37a6eef48dd2e93a17a7afb2a2c5f7572dce0839"} {"question" "q-117291" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057235/state.json" "file_sha256" "459b01f2922a2facdd7474498337b79a553fa0414a822134219f87714fad8561"} {"question" "q-117291" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-e"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2+5e"] ["math" "3-4{e}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057235/state.json" "file_sha256" "459b01f2922a2facdd7474498337b79a553fa0414a822134219f87714fad8561"} {"question" "q-117291" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-e"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057235/state.json" "file_sha256" "459b01f2922a2facdd7474498337b79a553fa0414a822134219f87714fad8561"} {"question" "q-117292" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find ${f}^{′}(0)$ if $f(x)={\\int }_{-{e}^{2x}}^{x}\\sqrt[3]{{t}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057235/state.json" "file_sha256" "459b01f2922a2facdd7474498337b79a553fa0414a822134219f87714fad8561"} {"question" "q-117292" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Here, both the lower and upper limits contain a variable. However, to apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit only. So, we can split the integral into two integrals, and rewrite them so that in both cases the variable is on the upper limit. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-{e}^{2x}}^{x}\\sqrt[3]{{t}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{-{e}^{2x}}^{a}\\sqrt[3]{{t}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{x}\\sqrt[3]{{t}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{a}^{-{e}^{2x}}\\sqrt[3]{{t}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{x}\\sqrt[3]{{t}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is any constant number between $-{e}^{2x}$ and $x.$\n\nThe integrand $\\sqrt[3]{{t}^{3}+1}$ is continuous for all $t,$ so applying the FTC II gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{\\int }_{a}^{-{e}^{2x}}\\sqrt[3]{{t}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{x}\\sqrt[3]{{t}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{-{e}^{2x}}\\sqrt[3]{{t}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}t)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{x}\\sqrt[3]{{t}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}({\\int }_{a}^{u}\\sqrt[3]{{t}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}t)+\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{3}+1} \\\\  & =-(-2{e}^{2x})\\sqrt[3]{{u}^{3}+1}+\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{3}+1} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x}\\sqrt[3]{(-{e}^{2x})^{3}+1}+\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{3}+1} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x}\\sqrt[3]{1-{e}^{6x}}+\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{3}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the substitution $u=-{e}^{2x}.$\n\nFinally, we can compute ${f}^{′}(0).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & =2{e}^{2(0)}\\sqrt[3]{1-{e}^{6(0)}}+\\sqrt[3]{{0}^{3}+1} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt[3]{1-1}+\\sqrt[3]{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057235/state.json" "file_sha256" "459b01f2922a2facdd7474498337b79a553fa0414a822134219f87714fad8561"} {"question" "q-117292" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057235/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "207539b50a0adc6d7c33b13e37a6eef48dd2e93a17a7afb2a2c5f7572dce0839"} {"question" "q-117292" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057235/state.json" "file_sha256" "459b01f2922a2facdd7474498337b79a553fa0414a822134219f87714fad8561"} {"question" "q-117292" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2\\sqrt[3]{1-e}+3"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "3\\sqrt[3]{1-e}-2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057235/state.json" "file_sha256" "459b01f2922a2facdd7474498337b79a553fa0414a822134219f87714fad8561"} {"question" "q-117292" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057235/state.json" "file_sha256" "459b01f2922a2facdd7474498337b79a553fa0414a822134219f87714fad8561"} {"question" "q-333583" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find ${g}^{′}(x)$ for $g(x)={\\int }_{{e}^{x}}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t.$\n\n${g}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057235/state.json" "file_sha256" "459b01f2922a2facdd7474498337b79a553fa0414a822134219f87714fad8561"} {"question" "q-333583" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute ${g}^{′}(x),$ we first make the substitution $u={e}^{x}$ and then we apply the chain rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{{e}^{x}}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{u}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit $u$ is located on the lower limit. To apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit. Using the properties of definite integrals, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{u}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(-{\\int }_{3}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{3}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by FTC II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{u}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{3}^{u}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(({e}^{x})^{2})) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{2x})) \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{2x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057235/state.json" "file_sha256" "459b01f2922a2facdd7474498337b79a553fa0414a822134219f87714fad8561"} {"question" "q-333583" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057235/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "207539b50a0adc6d7c33b13e37a6eef48dd2e93a17a7afb2a2c5f7572dce0839"} {"question" "q-333583" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057235/state.json" "file_sha256" "459b01f2922a2facdd7474498337b79a553fa0414a822134219f87714fad8561"} {"question" "q-333583" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-e^x\\sin(e^{2x})"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057235/state.json" "file_sha256" "459b01f2922a2facdd7474498337b79a553fa0414a822134219f87714fad8561"}] "usage" {"q-4555" []} "basis" 1846 "retractions" [[17592186078862 :question/problem "For $x > 2$, find $f^{′}(x)$ if $f(x) = ∫_{2}^{\\sqrt{x}}\\frac{1}{2 - t^{2}}dt$."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186076384 17592186076385 17592186076386 17592186076387 17592186076388] "immutable_fields" [[17592186076383 :answer-field/correct] [17592186076383 :answer-field/id] [17592186076383 :answer-field/key] [17592186076383 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
